{"iteration": 1, "system": "contrastive_error_memory", "avg_val": 36.0, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Showing errors with explicit wrong/correct contrast (\u2717 Wrong / \u2713 Correct) will outperform plain Q/A fewshot, because the model can learn what to avoid rather than only what to imitate.", "delta": -7.8, "outcome": "36.0% (-7.8)", "components": ["contrastive_pairs", "error_priority", "separated_pools"], "timing_s": {"propose": 248.2, "bench": 6765.6, "wall": 7027.6}, "propose_cost_usd": 0.940981, "solver_cost_usd": 0.2341}
{"iteration": 1, "system": "reflexion_memory", "avg_val": 29.3, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "LLM-distilled lessons from error batches will provide denser, more transferable guidance than raw fewshot examples, improving accuracy especially when the label space is large and examples are sparse per class.", "delta": -14.5, "outcome": "29.3% (-14.5)", "components": ["llm_synthesis", "lesson_distillation", "deduplication", "lesson_plus_examples"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.1983}
{"iteration": 2, "system": "label_balanced_memory", "avg_val": 44.7, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Round-robin selection across per-label buckets will match or exceed fewshot_all accuracy because it guarantees full label coverage at any char budget, whereas recency-based selection systematically under-represents labels that appear early or infrequently in training.", "delta": -0.9, "outcome": "44.7% (-0.9)", "components": ["label_balanced_selection", "round_robin", "coverage_maximization"], "timing_s": {"propose": 442.1, "bench": 5859.6, "wall": 6312.9}, "propose_cost_usd": 1.775112, "solver_cost_usd": 0.4533}
{"iteration": 2, "system": "similarity_retrieval_memory", "avg_val": 45.6, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Ranking stored examples by Jaccard token similarity to the current query at predict time will outperform recency-based selection on tasks with structured or domain-specific vocabulary (e.g. chemistry, legal), because more topically similar demonstrations improve answer quality more than recency does.", "delta": -0.0, "outcome": "45.6% (-0.0)", "components": ["jaccard_similarity", "query_time_retrieval", "token_overlap_ranking"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.4267}
{"iteration": 3, "system": "label_champion_memory", "avg_val": 46.2, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Picking the single most query-similar example per label (champion pass) and then filling remaining context with non-champion examples ranked by similarity will outperform both similarity_retrieval_memory (which ignores label coverage) and label_balanced_memory (which ignores per-example relevance), because it guarantees every seen label appears at least once while still surfacing the most topically relevant representative for each label.", "delta": 0.0, "outcome": "46.2% (+0.0)", "components": ["per_label_champion", "similarity_ranking", "coverage_guarantee", "fill_phase"], "timing_s": {"propose": 334.4, "bench": 5214.9, "wall": 5556.9}, "propose_cost_usd": 1.500318, "solver_cost_usd": 0.3911}
{"iteration": 3, "system": "mmr_memory", "avg_val": 45.8, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Greedy Maximum Marginal Relevance selection (lambda=0.6) will outperform pure similarity ranking because it penalises redundant examples at each step, naturally producing a diverse and relevant context set without requiring explicit label bookkeeping \u2014 reducing token waste from near-duplicate examples that pure Jaccard ranking tends to cluster.", "delta": -0.4, "outcome": "45.8% (-0.4)", "components": ["mmr", "relevance_diversity_tradeoff", "greedy_selection", "redundancy_penalty"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.4378}
{"iteration": 4, "system": "label_aware_prompt_memory", "avg_val": 43.8, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Prepending an explicit 'Valid answers' list of all seen labels to every prompt will improve accuracy on tasks with large label spaces, because the model currently only sees the labels that happen to appear in the retrieved examples \u2014 missing labels cannot be predicted regardless of how good the retrieval is.", "delta": -3.8, "outcome": "43.8% (-3.8)", "components": ["label_space_disclosure", "prompt_template_axis", "similarity_retrieval", "cold_start_fallback"], "timing_s": {"propose": 484.4, "bench": 9445.2, "wall": 9938.8}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.094514, "solver_cost_usd": 0.5171}
{"iteration": 4, "system": "confusion_disambiguation_memory", "avg_val": 47.6, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Tracking a confusion matrix during training and injecting stored examples of historically confused label pairs before similarity-ranked examples will outperform pure similarity retrieval, because surfacing the hard label boundaries the model has already failed on provides more targeted disambiguation signal than topically similar but label-redundant examples.", "delta": -0.0, "outcome": "47.6% (-0.0)", "components": ["confusion_matrix", "error_driven_selection", "disambiguation_examples", "learning_trigger_axis", "graceful_cold_start"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.2328}
{"iteration": 5, "system": "confusion_mmr_memory", "avg_val": 46.9, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Replacing the similarity-rank fill phase in confusion_disambiguation_memory with MMR fill will recover LawBench accuracy (34%+) while preserving the Symptom2Disease gain (90%), because the MMR redundancy penalty restores the diversity signal that pure similarity-rank fill loses after the disambiguation phase inserts topically similar boundary examples.", "delta": -0.7, "outcome": "46.9% (-0.7)", "components": ["confusion_matrix", "disambiguation_phase", "mmr_fill", "redundancy_penalty", "learning_trigger_axis"], "timing_s": {"propose": 527.8, "bench": 5341.9, "wall": 5877.6}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.146372, "solver_cost_usd": 0.4034}
{"iteration": 5, "system": "difficulty_weighted_mmr_memory", "avg_val": 40.9, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Applying a continuous per-label difficulty boost (error_rate * beta) directly inside the MMR relevance term will outperform plain mmr_memory and confusion_disambiguation_memory because it surfaces hard-to-predict labels through a graded unified scoring pass rather than binary slot reservation, producing better coverage of high-error labels across all datasets.", "delta": -6.7, "outcome": "40.9% (-6.7)", "components": ["per_label_error_rate", "difficulty_boost", "mmr_fill", "unified_scoring", "learning_trigger_axis"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.5042}
{"iteration": 6, "system": "ngram_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 47.3, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Replacing the ASCII word tokenizer in confusion_disambiguation_memory with character bigrams will raise LawBench accuracy above 34% (the mmr_memory frontier) while keeping Symptom2Disease at 90%, because bigrams give meaningful non-zero similarity scores for non-ASCII text whereas word tokens reduce to ~4 numerals per example and make retrieval indistinguishable from random.", "delta": -0.5, "outcome": "47.3% (-0.5)", "components": ["bigram_tokenizer", "language_agnostic_retrieval", "confusion_matrix", "disambiguation_phase", "similarity_fill"], "timing_s": {"propose": 494.2, "bench": 6176.3, "wall": 6678.9}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.377164, "solver_cost_usd": 0.3889}
{"iteration": 6, "system": "label_grouped_memory", "avg_val": 47.8, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Organising retrieved examples into per-label sections in the prompt (grouped by answer) will outperform the flat Q/A list used by all prior systems, because explicit label-boundary structure lets the model compare across categories rather than interpolate from a sequence of loosely ordered examples \u2014 particularly on tasks with many similar-looking inputs that map to different labels.", "delta": -0.0, "outcome": "47.8% (-0.0)", "components": ["bigram_tokenizer", "language_agnostic_retrieval", "label_grouped_prompt", "prompt_architecture_axis", "section_headers"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.4461}
{"iteration": 7, "system": "adaptive_tokenizer_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 51.6, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Routing to character bigrams for CJK text and word tokens for ASCII/Latin text will recover the USPTO and Symptom2Disease accuracy lost by ngram_confusion_memory while keeping LawBench at 38%, because the adaptive strategy avoids the mutual degradation caused by applying a single tokenizer to all scripts.", "delta": -0.0, "outcome": "51.6% (-0.0)", "components": ["adaptive_tokenizer", "cjk_detection", "bigram_cjk", "word_token_ascii", "confusion_matrix", "disambiguation_phase", "similarity_fill"], "timing_s": {"propose": 505.2, "bench": 4100.1, "wall": 4613.3}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.270363, "solver_cost_usd": 0.0}
{"iteration": 7, "system": "llm_rule_synthesis_memory", "avg_val": 44.0, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Synthesising and storing concise LLM-generated pairwise decision rules for high-confusion label pairs, then injecting those rules at the top of the predict prompt, will outperform pure example retrieval because it provides explicit boundary guidance that the model would otherwise have to infer from raw examples alone.", "delta": -7.6, "outcome": "44.0% (-7.6)", "components": ["adaptive_tokenizer", "confusion_matrix", "llm_rule_synthesis", "pairwise_rules", "rule_injection_prompt", "disambiguation_phase", "similarity_fill", "llm_usage_in_learning"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.4721}
{"iteration": 8, "system": "confusion_normalized_bfs_memory", "avg_val": 48.9, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Normalizing raw predictions before storing them in the confusion matrix (stripping format wrappers like '[TAG]label<eoa>') will unlock the disambiguation phase that has never fired in any prior system, because confusion keys currently have zero overlap with ground-truth labels. With normalization active, BFS depth-2 traversal with count/depth weight decay will additionally surface transitively confused label clusters missed by single-hop lookup, improving LawBench beyond 38%.", "delta": -2.7, "outcome": "48.9% (-2.7)", "components": ["prediction_normalization", "format_wrapper_stripping", "bfs_depth2", "weighted_traversal", "adaptive_tokenizer", "confusion_matrix"], "timing_s": {"propose": 773.0, "bench": 3668.5, "wall": 4450.9}, "propose_cost_usd": 3.233895, "solver_cost_usd": 0.1454}
{"iteration": 8, "system": "label_prototype_memory", "avg_val": 48.2, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Maintaining one LLM-written discriminative prototype per label (a 1-2 sentence description of what makes that label distinct from its confusors) and injecting the top-K most query-similar prototypes above few-shot examples will outperform pure example retrieval, because compressed categorical descriptions provide targeted boundary guidance the model would otherwise have to infer from raw examples alone \u2014 without the O(N^2) context cost that regressed llm_rule_synthesis_memory.", "delta": -3.4, "outcome": "48.2% (-3.4)", "components": ["per_label_prototype", "llm_synthesis", "incremental_update", "error_threshold_trigger", "prototype_similarity_retrieval", "memory_content_axis", "llm_usage_in_learning"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.3667}
{"iteration": 9, "system": "fixed_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 50.9, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Normalising raw model predictions before storing them as confusion-matrix keys (stripping wrappers like '[TAG]label<eoa>') will allow the disambiguation phase to fire for the first time in any confusion-based system, because prior systems stored keys in wrapped format that never matched the plain ground-truth label strings used during retrieval lookup, making the disambiguation phase permanently inactive.", "delta": -0.7, "outcome": "50.9% (-0.7)", "components": ["prediction_normalization", "wrapper_stripping", "adaptive_tokenizer", "confusion_matrix", "disambiguation_phase", "similarity_fill"], "timing_s": {"propose": 614.8, "bench": 3054.0, "wall": 3676.9}, "propose_cost_usd": 3.213013, "solver_cost_usd": 0.037}
{"iteration": 9, "system": "idf_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 48.4, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Replacing plain Jaccard similarity with IDF-weighted Jaccard (weighting each token by its inverse document frequency across stored examples) will improve retrieval quality by down-weighting ubiquitous filler tokens and up-weighting rare discriminative terms, on top of the prediction normalisation fix that unlocks the disambiguation phase.", "delta": -3.2, "outcome": "48.4% (-3.2)", "components": ["idf_weighted_jaccard", "lazy_idf_recompute", "prediction_normalization", "adaptive_tokenizer", "confusion_matrix", "disambiguation_phase", "similarity_fill"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.4235}
{"iteration": 10, "system": "anchor_last_memory", "avg_val": 46.0, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Placing the single best-matching example per top candidate label at the very end of the context window (immediately before the question) will outperform similarity_retrieval_memory and the current frontier, because transformer attention has a recency/proximity bias and examples closest to the question exert stronger influence on the prediction than examples buried at the top of a long context.", "delta": -5.6, "outcome": "46.0% (-5.6)", "components": ["anchor_at_end", "proximity_bias", "per_label_anchor", "adaptive_tokenizer", "fill_then_anchor", "prompt_architecture_axis"], "timing_s": {"propose": 620.8, "bench": 4847.1, "wall": 5476.4}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.606199, "solver_cost_usd": 0.4406}
{"iteration": 10, "system": "utility_weighted_memory", "avg_val": 45.8, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Weighting retrieval by a per-example utility score (accumulated +1/\u22121 based on whether each example was in context when a prediction was correct/wrong) will outperform pure similarity retrieval, because examples that have historically helped the model predict correctly are better demonstrations than topically similar examples that happened to mislead it.", "delta": -5.8, "outcome": "45.8% (-5.8)", "components": ["per_example_utility", "outcome_feedback", "sigmoid_modifier", "adaptive_tokenizer", "retrieved_indices_metadata", "memory_content_axis", "selection_algorithm_axis"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.3937}
{"iteration": 11, "system": "contrastive_boundary_memory", "avg_val": 46.7, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Rendering disambiguation examples in a dedicated 'Common Confusions' section with an explicit '[NOT: wrong_label]' annotation per example will outperform the frontier system, because it gives the model a direct negative-example signal at each label boundary rather than forcing it to infer the boundary from two similar positive examples buried in a flat list.", "delta": -4.9, "outcome": "46.7% (-4.9)", "components": ["contrastive_negative_annotation", "two_section_prompt", "disambiguation_header", "adaptive_tokenizer", "confusion_matrix", "prompt_architecture_axis"], "timing_s": {"propose": 636.8, "bench": 4656.2, "wall": 5306.5}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.845746, "solver_cost_usd": 0.4459}
{"iteration": 11, "system": "cluster_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 50.2, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Expanding the top candidate labels to their full connected component in the undirected confusion graph (BFS over both prediction\u2192actual and actual\u2192prediction edges) and injecting one representative per cluster member will outperform the frontier system, because it surfaces transitively-confused label boundaries that one-hop disambiguation misses \u2014 particularly on tasks where label confusions form chains (A\u2194B and B\u2194C) rather than isolated pairs.", "delta": -1.4, "outcome": "50.2% (-1.4)", "components": ["undirected_confusion_graph", "bfs_cluster_expansion", "transitive_confusion", "cluster_representative_injection", "adaptive_tokenizer", "selection_algorithm_axis"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.1971}
{"iteration": 12, "system": "anchor_recency_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 45.6, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Replacing the unbounded flat pool with an anchor dict (one example per label, always updated to most recent) plus a bounded recency window (80 examples) will improve LawBench accuracy by ensuring all 116 labels are always represented in the pool, while the frontier's unbounded pool (~200+ examples after training) under-represents late-arriving labels.", "delta": -6.0, "outcome": "45.6% (-6.0)", "components": ["anchor_pool", "recency_window", "confusion_disambiguation", "adaptive_jaccard"], "timing_s": {"propose": 1286.1, "bench": 4148.9, "wall": 5446.3}, "propose_cost_usd": 6.065998, "solver_cost_usd": 0.2748}
{"iteration": 12, "system": "discriminative_similarity_memory", "avg_val": 47.8, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Replacing flat Jaccard with label-discriminativeness-weighted Jaccard \u2014 where w(t) = 1/num_distinct_labels_containing_t \u2014 will improve retrieval precision on tasks with many labels and shared boilerplate tokens, because tokens exclusive to one or two labels will dominate the similarity score while tokens spread across all labels will be down-weighted.", "delta": -3.8, "outcome": "47.8% (-3.8)", "components": ["discriminative_weighting", "token_label_index", "confusion_disambiguation", "adaptive_jaccard"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.463}
{"iteration": 13, "system": "unified_confusion_retrieval_memory", "avg_val": 49.6, "axis": "exploitation", "hypothesis": "Replacing the two-phase confusion-slot injection with a single unified ranking that scores each example as jaccard(query, ex) + alpha*log(1+confusion_affinity(ex.label)) will outperform adaptive_tokenizer_confusion_memory, because confusion examples are only surfaced when they are also topically similar to the query, ending the pattern where fixed confusion slots displace more-relevant similarity examples from the context budget.", "delta": -2.6, "outcome": "49.6% (-2.6)", "components": ["unified_scoring", "confusion_affinity_bonus", "log_scaled_bonus", "adaptive_tokenizer", "prediction_normalization", "selection_algorithm_axis"], "timing_s": {"propose": 620.7, "bench": 2596.8, "wall": 3227.8}, "propose_cost_usd": 2.781396, "solver_cost_usd": 0.3486}
{"iteration": 13, "system": "decayed_confusion_memory", "avg_val": 52.2, "axis": "exploration", "hypothesis": "Multiplying all confusion counts by a decay factor (0.95) after each learn_from_batch call will outperform adaptive_tokenizer_confusion_memory by making the confusion matrix reflect the model's current error patterns rather than its full accumulated history, so label pairs that were confused early but later corrected no longer dominate disambiguation slot selection.", "delta": 0.0, "outcome": "52.2% (+0.0)", "components": ["exponential_decay", "float_confusion_counts", "count_pruning", "adaptive_tokenizer", "prediction_normalization", "learning_trigger_axis"], "solver_cost_usd": 0.0623}

